@clevelandwithcuz In George Orwell's 1984, surveillance is a central theme that underscores the oppressive control exerted by the Party over the citizens of Oceania. The novel presents a dystopian society where individuals are constantly monitored, leaving them in a state of perpetual fear and anxiety. This omnipresent surveillance serves as a tool for psychological manipulation, ensuring the Party's absolute power and control over the populace.

From the very beginning, the novel establishes a world where citizens are under constant watch. Winston Smith, the protagonist, lives in a society where telescreens monitor every action and sound. These devices cannot be turned off, ensuring that the Party can observe individuals at all times. This relentless surveillance extends even into private spaces, as Winston can only find a small nook in his apartment to write in his diary, hidden from the telescreen. The pervasive monitoring creates an environment of fear, where any action could lead to punishment by torture or death.
The Party's control through surveillance is further emphasized by the psychological impact it has on individuals. The constant fear of being watched leads to self-regulation, where citizens control their expressions and actions to avoid suspicion. This internalized control is a powerful tool for the Party, as it suppresses dissent and ensures loyalty. The anxiety caused by surveillance is compounded by the disappearance of individuals, known as "vaporization," and the expectation that family members and peers will report any disloyalty.

